// Cs5.grammar — C# 5.0 `async`/`await` (T3.4).
//
// `async` is a METHOD MODIFIER (like `static`, `partial`) and `await` is a UNARY PREFIX expression
// operator. Both are CONTEXTUAL KEYWORDS in Roslyn: plain identifiers in C# 1.0–4.0, keywords in
// C# 5.0+. They are therefore NOT added to `ReservedKeyword` (that would break C# 1.0–4.0 code that
// uses them as identifiers, e.g. `class C { int async; }` / `class C { int await; }`). Instead:
//   * `async` is APPENDED to the Cs1 `MethodModifier` union (T0.3 merge), so it is accepted only at
//     v5+.
//   * `await` is APPENDED to the Cs1 `Expression` TDOPP rule as a prefix operator (T0.3 merge), so
//     the await EXPRESSION is recognized only at v5+ (a bare `await` identifier still parses at every
//     version, since `await` is not reserved).
//
// T0.3 merge semantics: re-declaring a rule name APPENDS alternatives (declaration order across
// files); it does NOT modify the Cs1 alternative. The engine is longest-match-wins and an
// equal-length tie resolves to the FIRST alternative (declaration order, Parser.cs:296-316). So a
// re-declared Cs5 alternative is reachable only when it is MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE with the Cs1 one.
//
// Roslyn refs (C:\RSDN\roslyn, main, src/Compilers/CSharp/Portable):
//   * `async` is a CONTEXTUAL keyword (IdentifierToken + ContextualKind == AsyncKeyword):
//     GetModifierExcludingScoped (Parser/LanguageParser.cs:1329-1330) maps it to
//     DeclarationModifiers.Async in the generic ParseModifiers loop; ReconsiderTypeAsAsyncModifier
//     (LanguageParser.cs:3406-3427) re-reads a type-position `async` as a modifier. IDS_FeatureAsync
//     (Errors/MessageID.cs:95) requires LanguageVersion.CSharp5 (MessageID.cs:701); the check is a
//     BINDER concern (Symbols/Source/ModifierUtils.cs:116) — the PARSER accepts the form.
//   * `await` is a CONTEXTUAL keyword and a UNARY PREFIX operator: IsAwaitExpression
//     (LanguageParser.cs:11376-11427) disambiguates it; parseUnaryOrPrimaryExpression
//     (LanguageParser.cs:11488-11492) builds an AwaitExpression with operand
//     ParseSubExpression(GetPrecedence(SyntaxKind.AwaitExpression)), and GetPrecedence
//     (LanguageParser.cs:11299-11301) returns Precedence.Unary — the SAME level as the Cs1 prefix
//     operators `-`/`!`/`~` (Expression = "-" Expression : Unary, Cs1.grammar:636). The version gate
//     is a BINDER concern (Binder/Binder_Await.cs:22).
//   * Syntax tests: AsyncParsingTests.cs:38 (SimpleAsyncMethod, `async void M() { }`),
//     AsyncParsingTests.cs:283 (MethodAsyncVarAsync, `static async void M(object async) { async.F();
//     }`), ExpressionParsingTests.cs:3214 (`await Task.Delay()`), AwaitParsingTests.cs:33.
//
// CS5 only: no async iterators (CS8), no `await using` (CS8), no async main (CS7), no async lambdas
// (the lambda modifier is a separate construct; the lambda BODY `await` is covered by the await
// expression here since a lambda body is an Expression).

// === `async` method modifier (C# 5.0). Re-declares Cs1 MethodModifier; APPENDS "async". ===
// `async` is a plain identifier in C# 1.0–4.0 (a valid TypeName / name) but a method modifier in
// C# 5.0+, so it is NOT added to Cs1 ReservedKeyword (that would break C# 1.0–4.0, where `async` is
// a valid name, e.g. `class C { int async; }`). Appending here accepts `async` as a method modifier
// only at version 5+. The Cs1 Method rule (Cs1.grammar:265) uses this merged modifier union, so no
// re-declaration of Method is needed. `"async"` is a WordLiteral (RuleGenerator.cs:77-79) and
// matches whole-word (async, not asyncX). The same pattern as `partial` (T3.1.3) and `static` (Cs2).
// Consequence: at v4 `async` is not a method modifier, so `async void M() { }` REJECTS (parsed as a
// `Type` name `async`, then `void` (reserved) cannot be the method name); at v5 it PARSES.
MethodModifier =
    | "async";

// === `await` expression (C# 5.0). Re-declares Cs1 Expression; APPENDS the AwaitExpr prefix. ===
// `await` is a UNARY PREFIX operator (like the Cs1 `-`/`!`/`~`/`++`/`--` prefix operators, which are
// `"<op>" Expression : Unary`, Cs1.grammar:636-640). It is a PREFIX alternative (it starts with the
// "await" literal, NOT a self-Ref to Expression, so the TDOPP builder classifies it as a prefix,
// Parser.cs:137-149). The operand is `Expression : Unary` (a ReqRef with Precedence = the Unary
// level, RuleGenerator.cs:90-94): minPrecedence = Unary's binding power, so the operand binds tighter
// than all binary operators (Multiplicative and below) — `await x * y` = `(await x) * y` — and the
// Comma (binding power 1, the lowest) is NOT applicable, so the operand does NOT absorb a following
// `,` in a comma-separated context (e.g. an argument list). This is the TDOPP Comma fix (T3.3.2)
// handled by the `: Unary` precedence — no separate `Expression : Comma` is needed (the `: Unary`
// already excludes the Comma, which is a lower level).
//
// Disambiguation with the bare `await` identifier (longest-match, no equal-length tie):
//   * `await M()` — PrimaryExpr (IdentifierName, Cs1.grammar:682) matches `await` (5 chars); AwaitExpr
//     matches `await M()` (9 chars). Longest-match -> AwaitExpr. No tie.
//   * bare `await` (no operand) — AwaitExpr fails (no expression after `await`); PrimaryExpr ->
//     `await` (a plain identifier). No tie.
// `await` is NOT reserved, so it remains a valid IdentifierName at every version; the await
// EXPRESSION is reachable only at v5+ (this alternative is absent at v4). At v4 a bare `await x;`
// parses as a local declaration of type `await` named `x` (valid C# 1.0 — the T2.1.5 D2 finding), and
// `await N();` REJECTS (the `()` is not valid after a declarator or a bare identifier expression).
Expression =
    | AwaitExpr = "await" Expression : Unary;
